From ced4abe5c517ca0454f694f7847a1c8172eb7f8b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Paprocki <hi@rpaprocki.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 09:04:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 30/30] macos: one shell-safe name for the bundle and
 custom.txt app-name

Upstream (073447b) builds with the typed name stripped of spaces and puts the
typed name into custom.txt as app-name. RustDesk on macOS takes its runtime
name from that app-name, looks for itself at
/Applications/<app-name>.app/Contents/MacOS/<app-name> (is_installed, the
service it installs, the updater), pastes the name unquoted into /bin/sh (the
daemon plist, the install script) and updates only [A-Za-z0-9._-] names,
while the workflow names the bundle and its executable after the build name.
"Acme Support (Mac)" was built as AcmeSupport(Mac).app but ran as
"Acme Support (Mac)": never installed, and with brackets the daemon is a shell
syntax error even when the names match.

macOS builds now send one name as both, mac_app_name(): letters, digits and
. _ -, anything else becomes a hyphen. Spaces become hyphens as before 073447b
("Acme Support" -> Acme-Support), so a rebuilt client replaces the installed
one instead of landing next to it with a new ID. Android keeps upstream's
typed name; Windows 1.5.0 names are [A-Za-z0-9-] already (0023).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
